@h1 Links & media h1@

Two jobs live on this page: linking to somewhere else with the anchor node, and
embedding playable audio or video. Links work everywhere; media plays in HTML
output and degrades to plain text in print and Markdown, so reach for it when
HTML is your target.

@h2 Links h2@

The link text is the node's body; the address rides in a parameter. That is the
core rule — content in the body, metadata in parameters — and it keeps your prose
clean:

@@@pre
Visit @a |href https://example.org| the Example site a@ for the archive.
pre@@@

renders "Visit" followed by a link reading "the Example site", then the rest of
the sentence. The address never clutters the words the reader sees.

@h2 Opening in a new tab h2@

Add a target parameter to open the link in a new browsing context:

@@@pre
@a |href https://example.org| |target _blank| the archive, in a new tab a@
pre@@@

If you omit the address entirely, the link falls back to a harmless placeholder,
so a half-written link never breaks the build — but do fill it in before you
publish.

@h2 Audio and video h2@

Embed a media file with the audio or video node. Give it a source, and turn on
the player controls:

@@@pre
@audio |src ./bell.mp3| |controls true| audio@

@video |src ./tour.mp4| |controls true| video@
pre@@@

Each renders the matching HTML media element with its controls shown, so the
reader gets a play button, a scrubber, and a volume control in a browser.

@aside(class note) 💡 The controls flag needs a value. A bare
@@@code |controls| code@@@ is read as a positional parameter and emits _no_
controls; write @@@code |controls true| code@@@ (any value works) to get the
player controls. This is an easy one to trip on. aside@

@h2 A note on Markdown and PDF h2@

Links survive every output format. Audio and video are HTML features: in Markdown
there is no media element, and in a printed PDF a video cannot play. If your
document must work as Markdown or on paper, treat embedded media as an
HTML-only enhancement and make sure the surrounding prose still stands without
it.

@h2 Common mistakes h2@

@ul
@li _A bare controls flag._ Without a value it does nothing; write it with a
value (above). li@
@li _An email or at-sign in link text._ A literal at-sign in running text breaks,
as covered in @a |href /docs/write/prose/| Paragraphs & prose a@. For a mailto
link, put the address in the link's address parameter, never in the visible
text. li@
@li _Expecting media in Markdown or print._ It is HTML-only; the prose must stand
on its own in other formats. li@
ul@

